{
  "dom_id": "step-q335599",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "A high-speed printer in an office building has a maximum printing capacity of $640$ pages per minute; however, current production is below this maximum. Since the technician started monitoring, the printer produced $480$ and $560$ pages per minute at minutes $6$ and $12,$ respectively. What is the printer's printing rate at minute $36?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the printing production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in minutes.*",
  "worked_solution": "The rate at which the printing production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible printing production and its current printing production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the printer, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=640$ pages, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(640-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{640-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{640-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|640-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $640-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}640-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 640-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 640-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =640-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, at minute $6,$ the printing production was $480$ pages per minute. So, applying the condition $E(6)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(6) & =480 \\\\ 640-K{e}^{-6k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =160.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, at minute $12,$ the printing production was $560$ pages per minute. So, applying the condition $E(12)=560,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(12) & =560 \\\\ 640-K{e}^{-12k} & =560 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-6k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{160}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =2 \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-6\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} & =160 \\\\ K & =160{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\ K & =320\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=640-320{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of pages printed per minute at minute $t=36$ is\n\n$$\nE(36)=640-320{e}^{-(36/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}=635\\,\\text{pages per minute}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
